  "worked_solution": "The work-energy principle states that the work done by a resultant force on a particle equals the change in the particle's kinetic energy. That is,\n\n$$\nW=\u0394\\,\\text{KE}\n$$\n\nwhere the change in kinetic energy of a particle is given by\n\n$$\n\u0394\\,\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}m{v}_{0}^{2}\n$$\n\nand $m$ is the particle's mass, ${v}_{0}$ is its initial velocity, and ${v}_{1}$ is its final velocity.\n\nThe particle's velocity is given by $v(t)=5t,$ where $t$ is time in seconds. So, the particle's initial velocity, at $t=2$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{0}=v(2)=5(2)=10\\,m/s,\n$$\n\nand final velocity, at $t=4$, is\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=v(4)=5(4)=20\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the work-energy principle, the work done by the force between these times is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =\u0394\\,\\text{KE} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (20)^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1.5\\cdot (10)^{2} \\\\  & =300-75 \\\\  & =225\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
